::ENG2_U03_L02_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A nation that asks its people to vote, work, build, and serve must also prepare them to think. Public learning is not a gift for a few families; it is a foundation for the whole republic." Question: Which statement best expresses the central idea?{
~A. Only families should decide whether children learn.#Incorrect. The passage emphasizes public learning as important to the republic.
=B. Public education prepares citizens to participate in and strengthen the nation.#Correct. This captures the passage's main point about education and civic life.
~C. Work is more important than learning.#Incorrect. Work is one civic responsibility, but the passage says people must be prepared to think.
~D. Voting should happen in schools.#Incorrect. Voting is mentioned as a civic responsibility, not as an event held in schools.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A free press does more than print daily events. It gives citizens the information they need to judge public choices." Question: Which answer is the topic, not the central idea?{
=A. A free press#Correct. This names the subject but does not state the writer's main point about it.
~B. A free press helps citizens judge public choices.#Incorrect. This is a central idea because it makes a point about the topic.
~C. Newspapers serve the public by checking facts.#Incorrect. This is a developed idea, not just a topic.
~D. Information helps citizens evaluate leaders.#Incorrect. This is a central idea statement.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A road is more than packed earth and stone. It is the path by which a farmer brings food to market, a doctor reaches a sick child, and a letter travels from one county to another." Question: Which detail best develops the idea that roads connect communities?{
~A. Roads are made of packed earth and stone.#Incorrect. This is literal description and does not best develop community connection.
~B. The passage uses the word road.#Incorrect. Repeating the topic does not develop the central idea.
~C. The passage is written in complete sentences.#Incorrect. Sentence form is not evidence for the central idea.
=D. Farmers, doctors, and letters use roads to reach people and places.#Correct. These details show economic, health, and communication connections.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "If officials act wisely, reports can explain the reasons and results. If officials act poorly, reports can reveal the harm and demand answers." Question: How does this detail develop the central idea about a free press?{
~A. It says the press should only praise officials.#Incorrect. The passage includes both wise and poor actions.
=B. It shows the press helps citizens understand and evaluate public actions.#Correct. The detail shows reporting can explain good choices and expose harmful ones.
~C. It says citizens should avoid public issues.#Incorrect. The passage gives citizens information to judge public choices.
~D. It proves officials never act poorly.#Incorrect. The passage directly mentions officials acting poorly.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "Public learning is not a gift for a few families; it is a foundation for the whole republic." Question: Which paraphrase is most accurate?{
~A. Public learning should serve only a few families.#Incorrect. This reverses the meaning.
~B. Learning is not connected to the republic.#Incorrect. The passage calls learning a foundation for the republic.
=C. Education should be broad because it supports the whole nation.#Correct. This restates the idea accurately.
~D. Families should reject public schools.#Incorrect. The passage supports public learning.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A newspaper should not serve only the powerful or the popular. It should serve the public by asking careful questions, checking facts, and making important issues visible." Question: Which central idea is developed?{
~A. Newspapers should entertain powerful people.#Incorrect. The passage says newspapers should not serve only the powerful or popular.
~B. Newspapers should avoid careful questions.#Incorrect. Careful questions are listed as part of public service.
~C. Newspapers should hide important issues.#Incorrect. The passage says they should make issues visible.
=D. Newspapers should serve the public through questioning, fact-checking, and visibility.#Correct. This states the main idea developed by the listed responsibilities.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "When children learn to read, reason, and speak with care, they become neighbors who can solve common problems." Question: How does this sentence develop the central idea?{
=A. It explains how education benefits the community, not only the individual child.#Correct. The detail connects student learning to solving shared problems.
~B. It proves reading is the only useful skill.#Incorrect. The sentence also includes reasoning and speaking.
~C. It argues that neighbors should avoid schools.#Incorrect. The sentence shows schools help communities.
~D. It says common problems cannot be solved.#Incorrect. It says educated neighbors can solve common problems.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A free press does more than print daily events." Question: What does this opening sentence prepare the reader to understand?{
~A. The passage will only list daily events.#Incorrect. It says the press does more than that.
=B. The passage will explain a larger civic role for the press.#Correct. The sentence introduces the idea that the press has a public purpose.
~C. The passage will reject newspapers completely.#Incorrect. The passage values a free press.
~D. The passage will focus on weather reports.#Incorrect. Daily events are mentioned generally, not as the main focus.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A community that repairs its roads repairs the connections among its people." Question: Which best states the central idea?{
~A. Roads are only useful for wagons.#Incorrect. The passage presents roads as broader community connections.
~B. Communities should stop repairing roads.#Incorrect. The passage supports road repair.
=C. Road repair can strengthen the social and practical connections in a community.#Correct. This captures the meaning of the sentence.
~D. Roads are less important than letters.#Incorrect. Letters are one example of what roads help move.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "Reports can reveal the harm and demand answers." Question: What does the phrase "demand answers" mean in context?{
~A. Ask for entertainment news#Incorrect. The context is public responsibility.
=B. Require explanations from those responsible#Correct. The phrase means the press can press officials to explain harmful actions.
~C. Avoid difficult questions#Incorrect. The phrase means the opposite.
~D. Print only private letters#Incorrect. The passage concerns public choices.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A child may walk past a schoolhouse and still be kept from its lessons by distance, cost, or custom." Question: How does this detail develop the passage?{
~A. It shows that schoolhouses are never useful.#Incorrect. The detail shows access barriers, not that schools are useless.
=B. It gives examples of barriers that prevent equal access to education.#Correct. Distance, cost, and custom show why public learning must be supported.
~C. It says children dislike learning.#Incorrect. The passage does not discuss children's preferences.
~D. It proves only distance matters.#Incorrect. Cost and custom are also named.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "It should serve the public by asking careful questions, checking facts, and making important issues visible." Question: What structure is used in this sentence to develop the central idea?{
=A. A list of responsibilities#Correct. The sentence lists ways the press serves the public.
~B. A fictional plot twist#Incorrect. This is informational writing, not fiction.
~C. A comparison of two rivers#Incorrect. No rivers are compared.
~D. A time sequence from morning to night#Incorrect. The sentence does not organize events by time.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "Public learning is not a gift for a few families; it is a foundation for the whole republic." Question: Which answer explains the contrast in the sentence?{
=A. It contrasts private benefit with public importance.#Correct. The sentence rejects education as a privilege for a few and presents it as important to everyone.
~B. It contrasts reading with writing.#Incorrect. Reading and writing are not contrasted here.
~C. It contrasts two kinds of weather.#Incorrect. Weather is not discussed.
~D. It contrasts schools with roads.#Incorrect. Roads are not in this passage.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "If officials act wisely, reports can explain the reasons and results. If officials act poorly, reports can reveal the harm and demand answers." Question: Which detail supports accountability?{
~A. Officials act wisely#Incorrect. This detail supports explanation, but accountability is clearer when poor actions are revealed.
~B. Daily events are printed#Incorrect. This phrase is not in this excerpt and is less specific.
=C. Reports can reveal harm and demand answers#Correct. This directly supports the idea that the press holds officials accountable.
~D. Newspapers are popular#Incorrect. Popularity is not the focus.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A nation that asks its people to vote, work, build, and serve must also prepare them to think." Question: What idea does this sentence develop?{
=A. Citizens need education to fulfill public responsibilities.#Correct. The sentence connects civic duties with the need to think.
~B. Citizens should never work.#Incorrect. Work is listed as a responsibility.
~C. Voting does not require thought.#Incorrect. The sentence argues people must be prepared to think.
~D. Only builders need schools.#Incorrect. Several responsibilities are listed.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A road is the path by which a farmer brings food to market, a doctor reaches a sick child, and a letter travels from one county to another." Question: Why does the speaker include three examples?{
~A. To avoid developing the central idea#Incorrect. The examples develop it.
~B. To prove roads are only for doctors#Incorrect. Farmers and letters are also included.
~C. To make the passage about farming only#Incorrect. Farming is one example.
=D. To show that roads affect trade, health, and communication#Correct. The examples develop the central idea by showing several public benefits.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A free press does more than print daily events. It gives citizens the information they need to judge public choices." Question: Which sentence best analyzes central idea development?{
=A. The second sentence develops the first by explaining the civic purpose of a free press.#Correct. It moves from a general claim to the reason the press matters.
~B. The second sentence changes the topic to farming.#Incorrect. Farming is not discussed.
~C. The first sentence says newspapers are useless.#Incorrect. It says they do more than print events.
~D. The passage avoids central ideas.#Incorrect. It clearly develops one.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "When roads fail, the work of many citizens is slowed. When roads are kept open and safe, trade, health, and friendship move with them." Question: What central idea is supported?{
~A. Roads affect only one person.#Incorrect. The passage says many citizens are affected.
~B. Roads have no public value.#Incorrect. The passage shows public value.
=C. Roads support the movement of goods, care, and relationships.#Correct. The sentence connects roads to trade, health, and friendship.
~D. Roads should always be closed.#Incorrect. The passage values open and safe roads.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "Therefore, communities should support schools that welcome every child and strengthen the future of all." Question: What role does this sentence play?{
=A. It gives the passage's call for action based on the central idea.#Correct. It tells what communities should do because education benefits all.
~B. It changes the topic to sports.#Incorrect. The sentence remains about schools and community.
~C. It denies that schools matter.#Incorrect. It says schools strengthen the future.
~D. It only describes a building's color.#Incorrect. No color is described.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A newspaper should not serve only the powerful or the popular." Question: What does this sentence add to the central idea?{
~A. It argues against all newspapers.#Incorrect. The passage argues for a responsible press.
~B. It says powerful people never read newspapers.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
~C. It says popularity is the only goal.#Incorrect. The sentence rejects serving only the popular.
=D. It explains that the press has a responsibility to the public, not just influential groups.#Correct. This develops the idea of public service.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "A free press does more than print daily events." Question: Which answer is a weak central idea because it is too narrow?{
~A. The passage says a free press has a public responsibility.#Incorrect. This is broad enough and accurate.
~B. The passage argues that public information helps citizens judge choices.#Incorrect. This is a strong central idea.
=C. The passage is only about printing daily events.#Correct. This is too narrow and ignores the phrase "does more than."
~D. The passage presents the press as a civic institution.#Incorrect. This is an appropriate central idea.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "When children learn to read, reason, and speak with care, they become neighbors who can solve common problems." Question: Which academic vocabulary word best fits what the sentence provides?{
~A. Contradiction#Incorrect. The sentence does not oppose itself.
=B. Supporting detail#Correct. The sentence supports the central idea about public education.
~C. Fictional setting#Incorrect. This is an informational speech passage.
~D. Rhyme#Incorrect. The sentence is not using rhyme as the main feature.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "A road is more than packed earth and stone." Question: How does this sentence begin developing the central idea?{
=A. It signals that the passage will explain a deeper public meaning of roads.#Correct. It moves the reader beyond a literal definition.
~B. It proves roads are imaginary.#Incorrect. The sentence says roads are more than their materials, not unreal.
~C. It says roads are only stones.#Incorrect. It says roads are more than that.
~D. It ends the argument completely.#Incorrect. It introduces the argument.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Essay Passage: "It gives citizens the information they need to judge public choices." Question: Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Information should be kept from citizens.#Incorrect. The passage values giving information to citizens.
~B. The press prevents citizens from thinking.#Incorrect. The passage says information helps citizens judge.
~C. Citizens should ignore public choices.#Incorrect. The passage encourages judgment of public choices.
=D. The press gives citizens information so they can evaluate decisions made in public life.#Correct. This restates the meaning accurately.
}

::ENG2_U03_L02_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.R.06] Historical Speech Passage: "Communities should support schools that welcome every child and strengthen the future of all." Question: Which evidence from the lesson passage best supports this final claim?{
~A. "A child may walk past a schoolhouse."#Incorrect. This begins the access problem but is not the strongest support for the public benefit.
=B. "When children learn to read, reason, and speak with care, they become neighbors who can solve common problems."#Correct. This detail explains why educating children strengthens the whole community.
~C. "In many towns" #Incorrect. This phrase gives setting but not a reason for the claim.
~D. "A few families" #Incorrect. This phrase alone is incomplete and does not develop the final claim.
}
